title: The query is the walk state
tags: [[Hierarchical tags]]
kind: rationale
audience: dev
aliases: gui-tag-folder-rework
summary: gui-tag-folder.sh holds no list: each question is the first answer of "scope and not yet decided", since every answer is already a write.

`gui-tag-folder.sh` used to read its whole scope before the first question — seven
reads, the walk order computed by an external `sort`, everything held in bash arrays and
capped at `MF_GUI_MAX_ENTRIES`. The rework (September 2026) starts from one observation:
an answer is already a ''write''. So "what is left to ask" is a query, not bookkeeping:

```
<scope> AND <not yet decided> AND <not skipped>
```

Each step asks for its first match (`--limit 1`), and each answer removes its subject —
a `y`/`n` on a folder writes the tag over `(<scope>) AND mfr_path ->* "P"` for its path P, so the
whole subtree leaves the set at once. Nothing is read up front, nothing is held, there
is no cap; a question costs two or three small round-trips, each behind a keypress.

!! Walk order

The only state is ''the current folder'': the path is the stack, and going up is
trimming one component. At folder P:

# the first undecided direct child in scope — folders first, then files, each by `order_dir`/`order_file` then path (so a numbered album is walked by track);
# none? the first undecided in-scope ''descendant'' of P, by path — the walk moves to its parent without asking;
# neither? up one component; above the root, the walk is done.

Rule 2 is what reaches a scattered scope (`/a` out of scope, `/a/b/c.txt` in it):
sorting by path is sorting depth first, so the first descendant is the one the walk
should reach next, and every empty level is skipped in one step. There is no sort by
depth to lean on instead: a `tree_ref` sort key orders on the whole path
([[Pagination and sorting]]). The repository root is nobody's child, so at the walk root
the folder query also asks for the forest root (`mfr_path:parent = "" OR mfr_path:parent
IS ABSENT`). A leaf is "not a folder", not `mfr_type = "file"`: a symlink is neither,
and a leaf query that missed it looped for ever without waiting for a key.

!! Skips and the counter

A skip is recorded, so it survives the run: `gui_tag_skipped`, a `ref` to the tag entry
— the same shape as `tag`, one row per tag, so a skip for `music` does not skip for
`jazz`, and the query needs one clause whatever their number. It is taken out ''by
value'' (`field remove`), since `unset` would drop every tag's marker. When the tag entry
does not exist yet, the skip holds for this run only, in a `uuid_in` list.

"N left" is a `count` over the same predicate — exact, O(1) on the index. A skipped
folder's subtree is still undecided, so both the count and the step query carry a
`NOT mfr_path ->* "P"` per skipped folder P: rule 2 would otherwise walk into it.

`--redo` drops the decided and skipped clauses, so nothing an answer writes narrows the
query; it keeps its own memory — the entries asked, the folders answered whole — bounded
by keypresses and restored by `back`.

The unit suite drives the hermetic `mf` mock ([[Testing shipped scripts]]) by declaring the walk as the ''successive
answers'' to the step queries (queues that empty as the real set would); the semantics
against a real daemon stay pinned by `scripts/test-scripts-integration.sh`.
